1
00:00:00,875 --> 00:00:04,575
You can move quickly and still lose track of what your team actually agreed to

2
00:00:04,575 --> 00:00:05,350
build.

3
00:00:06,970 --> 00:00:08,983
A requirement sits in a document.

4
00:00:08,983 --> 00:00:10,995
A new request arrives later.

5
00:00:10,995 --> 00:00:14,770
By the time work reaches a coding agent, those two things can look like the same

6
00:00:14,770 --> 00:00:15,595
plan.

7
00:00:17,190 --> 00:00:20,465
Orchestra keeps those different kinds of context connected.

8
00:00:22,060 --> 00:00:24,773
We're using Northstar, a fictional launch project.

9
00:00:24,773 --> 00:00:27,048
Start in Memory with the original PRD.

10
00:00:27,048 --> 00:00:30,710
Its CSV export lists four fields: item ID,

11
00:00:30,710 --> 00:00:32,635
title, owner and status.

12
00:00:32,635 --> 00:00:35,085
The source stays available to inspect.

13
00:00:36,705 --> 00:00:40,930
Now ask Socrates: what is in scope, is PDF export approved,

14
00:00:40,930 --> 00:00:43,105
and do we have a launch date or budget?

15
00:00:44,700 --> 00:00:49,025
With OpenAI configured, Socrates generates an answer from the available

16
00:00:49,025 --> 00:00:50,413
project evidence.

17
00:00:50,413 --> 00:00:53,450
Open a citation and check the underlying document.

18
00:00:53,450 --> 00:00:55,850
The useful result is not just a summary.

19
00:00:55,850 --> 00:00:57,875
It's a distinction you can verify.

20
00:00:59,520 --> 00:01:02,358
CSV export is documented in the PRD.

21
00:01:02,358 --> 00:01:06,695
PDF export appears as a later request, with no recorded approval

22
00:01:06,695 --> 00:01:08,570
established by this evidence.

23
00:01:08,570 --> 00:01:12,395
The available sources do not establish a launch date or budget.

24
00:01:13,990 --> 00:01:16,690
That gives your team a more useful next question:

25
00:01:16,690 --> 00:01:19,515
should PDF export become part of this launch,

26
00:01:19,515 --> 00:01:20,990
and who needs to decide?

27
00:01:22,610 --> 00:01:25,073
Some questions need more than a chat response.

28
00:01:25,073 --> 00:01:28,210
Deep Research takes a broader question and produces a report linked to its

29
00:01:28,210 --> 00:01:29,098
sources.

30
00:01:29,098 --> 00:01:33,735
Here, the saved report covers CSV scope and the PDF request,

31
00:01:33,735 --> 00:01:36,623
while keeping the missing launch date and budget visible.

32
00:01:36,623 --> 00:01:40,060
Open its source links to inspect the evidence behind the findings.

33
00:01:40,060 --> 00:01:42,810
Saving the report preserves generated evidence;

34
00:01:42,810 --> 00:01:45,160
it does not create an approved requirement.

35
00:01:46,805 --> 00:01:51,543
Now open Truth Inbox, where proposed changes and review signals come together.

36
00:01:51,543 --> 00:01:55,443
A reviewer can inspect the evidence and affected areas before deciding.

37
00:01:55,443 --> 00:01:58,768
In this project, PDF remains a request.

38
00:01:58,768 --> 00:02:02,430
Review separates a new idea from an accepted product decision.

39
00:02:04,025 --> 00:02:08,063
Before an agent starts implementation, Agent Preflight assembles a scoped pack

40
00:02:08,063 --> 00:02:12,813
with project evidence, constraints, an acceptance checklist and open questions.

41
00:02:12,813 --> 00:02:14,225
This pack is blocked.

42
00:02:14,225 --> 00:02:17,913
The missing tests are visible, and the project has no recorded human-approved

43
00:02:17,913 --> 00:02:18,988
requirements.

44
00:02:18,988 --> 00:02:22,725
That's useful context: the next action is to resolve the gap,

45
00:02:22,725 --> 00:02:24,500
not assume the work is ready.

46
00:02:26,095 --> 00:02:27,708
The handoff is specific.

47
00:02:27,708 --> 00:02:32,470
Pair Codex or VS Code, and use Orchestra MCP to retrieve the exact

48
00:02:32,470 --> 00:02:34,045
project and pack ID.

49
00:02:34,045 --> 00:02:36,895
A retrieved pack is still subject to its blockers.

50
00:02:36,895 --> 00:02:40,145
It is context for the agent, not permission to proceed.

51
00:02:41,765 --> 00:02:44,640
Orchestra helps a fast-moving team keep its questions,

52
00:02:44,640 --> 00:02:46,978
decisions and implementation connected.

53
00:02:46,978 --> 00:02:48,215
Start with the source.

54
00:02:48,215 --> 00:02:49,553
Review the change.

55
00:02:49,553 --> 00:02:52,165
Give your agents context the team can trace.

56
00:02:53,760 --> 00:02:54,360
Orchestra.

57
00:02:54,360 --> 00:02:56,510
Product Brain for High Speed Teams.
